WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.190 --> 00:00:05.480
The Caped Crusader's ongoing film saga ranks
among the top five highest-grossing movie

00:00:05.480 --> 00:00:07.700
franchises in the United States.

00:00:07.700 --> 00:00:11.040
But he's also faced his share of setbacks
along the way.

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:15.849
For every Bat-blockbuster that made its way
to theaters, there's a take on the character

00:00:15.849 --> 00:00:19.070
that unceremoniously died in development.

00:00:19.070 --> 00:00:23.070
Here's the history behind every one of those
canceled Batman movies.

00:00:23.070 --> 00:00:24.070
The Batman

00:00:24.070 --> 00:00:29.580
Getting Tim Burton's 1989 Batman onto the
big screen was a difficult process.

00:00:29.580 --> 00:00:35.239
Ten years before Michael Keaton made his Dark
Knight debut, producers Benjamin Melniker

00:00:35.239 --> 00:00:39.899
and Michael Uslan acquired the film rights
to Batman so they could adapt the character

00:00:39.899 --> 00:00:42.059
in the same vein as Superman.

00:00:42.059 --> 00:00:47.479
They wanted to have a definitive, dark version
of Batman on film, and producers Jon Peters

00:00:47.479 --> 00:00:52.620
and Peter Guber agreed when they boarded the
project in November 1979.

00:00:52.620 --> 00:00:58.120
Four years later, the script by James Bond
and Superman screenplay co-writer Tom Mankiewicz,

00:00:58.120 --> 00:01:04.440
simply titled The Batman, was completed, focusing
on Batman and Robin's origins, with the Joker

00:01:04.440 --> 00:01:05.840
as the central villain.

00:01:05.840 --> 00:01:11.250
While the Batman/007 version of Batman would
have been awesome, the project went through

00:01:11.250 --> 00:01:15.710
multiple developers and producers, losing
bits and pieces along the way.

00:01:15.710 --> 00:01:20.860
In the end, the studio hired Tim Burton to
direct, who felt the treatment was too similar

00:01:20.860 --> 00:01:24.480
to Superman and would be a better fit for
a TV series.

00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:29.830
He changed virtually every aspect of the movie,
so while they didn't get everything they wanted,

00:01:29.830 --> 00:01:35.430
the producers still got their darker version
of Batman on the silver screen.

00:01:35.430 --> 00:01:38.270
It just took a decade to make it happen.

00:01:38.270 --> 00:01:39.520
Robin's spinoff

00:01:39.520 --> 00:01:45.000
When Chris O'Donnell joined the franchise
as Robin for 1995's Batman Forever, he seemed

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:47.510
poised for a long run as the character.

00:01:47.510 --> 00:01:52.700
He returned for 1997's Batman &amp; Robin, and
planned to reprise his role in a possible

00:01:52.700 --> 00:01:55.630
fifth film, but that never came to fruition.

00:01:55.630 --> 00:02:01.070
Warner Bros. also briefly pursued a Robin
spinoff, which O'Donnell confirmed as recently

00:02:01.070 --> 00:02:02.480
as 2012.

00:02:02.480 --> 00:02:03.870
As he told Access Hollywood,

00:02:03.870 --> 00:02:07.290
"They were going to do [a Robin movie] back
in the day.

00:02:07.290 --> 00:02:12.340
Then [Batman &amp; Robin] was such a bomb, they
were like, 'Scrap that!'

00:02:12.340 --> 00:02:14.060
That was the end of that."

00:02:14.060 --> 00:02:15.120
Batman Unchained

00:02:15.120 --> 00:02:20.200
Joel Schumacher told Variety that he'd always
planned to make three Batman movies.

00:02:20.200 --> 00:02:26.170
But unfortunately for everyone involved in
Schumacher's Bat-saga, Batman &amp; Robin tanked,

00:02:26.170 --> 00:02:32.200
sending the franchise into years of development
hell, and leaving that third movie unrealized.

00:02:32.200 --> 00:02:34.730
The problems weren't just at the box office
however.

00:02:34.730 --> 00:02:39.030
Akiva Goldsman, who wrote the screenplays
for Schumacher's first two Bat-flicks, opted

00:02:39.030 --> 00:02:40.800
not to return for the next installment.

00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:46.200
I Am Legend screenwriter Mark Protosevich
took Goldsman's place and came up with the

00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:51.410
name Batman Unchained, which focused on Scarecrow
as the central villain.

00:02:51.410 --> 00:02:57.120
Schumacher wanted Nicolas Cage play the fear-inducing
bad guy, saying that he owed the world, quote,

00:02:57.120 --> 00:02:59.070
"a real Batman movie."

00:02:59.070 --> 00:03:03.540
But Batman &amp; Robin crashed and burned, taking
Batman Unchained with it.

00:03:03.540 --> 00:03:08.790
Changing course completely, Warner Bros. started
looking into the idea of making a Batman Beyond

00:03:08.790 --> 00:03:11.900
or Batman: Year One film instead.

00:03:11.900 --> 00:03:13.770
Batman: DarKnight

00:03:13.770 --> 00:03:18.670
Writers Lee Shapiro and Stephen Wise had an
idea for a full Bat-reboot.

00:03:18.670 --> 00:03:23.819
This time, instead of Batman and Robin in
their primes, the duo wanted to see Batman

00:03:23.819 --> 00:03:26.390
retired, with Dick Grayson attending college.

00:03:26.390 --> 00:03:31.380
In the proposed film, Dr. Jonathan Crane,
better known as the Scarecrow, still played

00:03:31.380 --> 00:03:35.540
a prominent role as a professor of psychology
at Gotham University.

00:03:35.540 --> 00:03:36.540
Also appearing?

00:03:36.540 --> 00:03:40.740
Dr. Kirk Langstrom, who turns into the monstrous
Man-Bat.

00:03:40.740 --> 00:03:46.040
The two doctors get into a confrontation,
and Crane accidentally initiates Kirk's transformation

00:03:46.040 --> 00:03:47.810
into his bat-like form.

00:03:47.810 --> 00:03:52.190
After seeing Man-Bat terrorize Gotham and
—that the city's residents are mistaking

00:03:52.190 --> 00:03:56.599
him for the real Batman — the Dark Knight
comes out of retirement to clear his name.

00:03:56.599 --> 00:04:01.110
The darker idea was pitched to the studio,
but after being passed through multiple levels

00:04:01.110 --> 00:04:06.569
of approval and a few confirmations from producers,
the studio's director of development passed

00:04:06.569 --> 00:04:07.750
on the movie.

00:04:07.750 --> 00:04:09.330
Batman: Year One

00:04:09.330 --> 00:04:14.970
Schumacher still wanted to give Batman fans
a proper Caped Crusader, and his planned adaptation

00:04:14.970 --> 00:04:19.570
of Frank Miller's Batman: Year One might have
fulfilled that goal.

00:04:19.570 --> 00:04:25.570
Ultimately, the studio parted ways with Schumacher
and went with Requiem for a Dream director

00:04:25.570 --> 00:04:27.960
Darren Aronofsky instead.

00:04:27.960 --> 00:04:32.490
After hiring Aronofsky, the studio bolstered
the project's pedigree further by bringing

00:04:32.490 --> 00:04:35.370
Frank Miller himself on board to script.

00:04:35.370 --> 00:04:39.270
In an interview with IGN, Aronofsky urged
fans to, quote,

00:04:39.270 --> 00:04:42.060
"...toss out everything you can imagine about
Batman!

00:04:42.060 --> 00:04:43.060
Everything!

00:04:43.060 --> 00:04:44.590
We're starting completely anew."

00:04:44.590 --> 00:04:49.020
Unfortunately, the studio didn't get into
Miller's version of the script, so they hired

00:04:49.020 --> 00:04:52.470
the Wachowskis of Matrix fame to write a treatment.

00:04:52.470 --> 00:04:57.669
Again, the studio wasn't satisfied, so they
turned to Joss Whedon, whose take also didn't

00:04:57.669 --> 00:04:59.440
pass muster with the studio.

00:04:59.440 --> 00:05:04.030
They finally ended up shelving the idea altogether
in 2002.

00:05:04.030 --> 00:05:05.380
Batman Beyond

00:05:05.380 --> 00:05:11.190
Warner Bros. also floated the idea of making
a Batman Beyond film inspired by the hit animated

00:05:11.190 --> 00:05:14.199
show, which had debuted in 1999.

00:05:14.199 --> 00:05:19.190
Bruce Timm and Paul Dini's series featured
Terry McGinnis, a teenager who takes over

00:05:19.190 --> 00:05:22.979
the mantle of Batman under the guidance of
a retired Bruce Wayne.

00:05:22.979 --> 00:05:28.150
The studio's movie division liked Batman Beyond
so much that they wanted to make a live-action

00:05:28.150 --> 00:05:30.290
version for the big screen.

00:05:30.290 --> 00:05:35.590
They hired Remember the Titans director Boaz
Yakin to helm the project, with Dini writing

00:05:35.590 --> 00:05:36.590
the script.

00:05:36.590 --> 00:05:41.610
Sources told Variety in 2000 that a Batman
Beyond film didn't interfere with Warner Bros.'

00:05:41.610 --> 00:05:46.150
plans to continue with a fifth Batman movie,
but that changed quickly.

00:05:46.150 --> 00:05:51.410
By August 2001, Dini said that the studio
had shelved the live-action adaptation.

00:05:51.410 --> 00:05:56.290
What's more, later that year the Batman Beyond
animated series was cancelled.

00:05:56.290 --> 00:05:58.639
Batman vs. Superman: Asylum

00:05:58.639 --> 00:06:04.300
Long before Zack Snyder's Batman v Superman:
Dawn of Justice hit the big screen, Warner

00:06:04.300 --> 00:06:10.660
Bros. previously had plans to pit the DC Universe's
two biggest heroes against each other.

00:06:10.660 --> 00:06:15.669
Andrew Kevin Walker, who penned the incredibly
dark Se7en, pitched an idea that involved

00:06:15.669 --> 00:06:21.360
Batman coming out of retirement, shortly after
his wife's death, to join a recently-divorced

00:06:21.360 --> 00:06:22.360
Superman.

00:06:22.360 --> 00:06:26.070
Until, of course, they're duped by Lex Luthor
into fighting each other.

00:06:26.070 --> 00:06:27.120
Sound familiar?

00:06:27.120 --> 00:06:32.580
Akiva Goldsman, returning to the franchise
once more, revised Walker's script in 2002,

00:06:32.580 --> 00:06:36.669
which was enough to convince Warner Bros.
to move forward with the project.

00:06:36.669 --> 00:06:39.130
At least for a little while.

00:06:39.130 --> 00:06:44.340
Wolfgang Petersen, director of Air Force One
and The NeverEnding Story, intended to direct

00:06:44.340 --> 00:06:45.630
the film in 2003.

00:06:45.630 --> 00:06:51.070
But the project was derailed when JJ Abrams
jumped into the fray with a script for a movie

00:06:51.070 --> 00:06:57.220
called Superman: Flyby, which included a very
different version of the Man of Steel.

00:06:57.220 --> 00:07:02.410
According to Petersen, the studio decided
to try separate versions of Superman and Batman

00:07:02.410 --> 00:07:07.639
instead, resulting in Superman Returns and
Batman Begins.

00:07:07.639 --> 00:07:08.700
Justice League: Mortal

00:07:08.700 --> 00:07:15.370
2017's Justice League wasn't the first attempt
to bring Batman and the rest of the "league"

00:07:15.370 --> 00:07:17.000
to the big screen.

00:07:17.000 --> 00:07:22.560
In 2009, the first plan to bring D.C.'s heroes
together came awfully close to hitting theaters.

00:07:22.560 --> 00:07:27.560
Written by Kieran and Michele Mulroney, Justice
League: Mortal was to be helmed by Mad Max

00:07:27.560 --> 00:07:29.510
director George Miller.

00:07:29.510 --> 00:07:31.210
Its cast included D.J.

00:07:31.210 --> 00:07:36.919
Cotrona as Superman, Armie Hammer as Batman,
Megan Gale as Wonder Woman, Adam Brody as

00:07:36.919 --> 00:07:43.380
the Flash, Common as Green Lantern, and Teresa
Palmer as Talia al Ghul, with Jay Baruchel

00:07:43.380 --> 00:07:45.310
playing the villain Maxwell Lord.

00:07:45.310 --> 00:07:50.400
Cameras were ready to roll, but the writers'
strike of the late 2000s put a damper on the

00:07:50.400 --> 00:07:51.400
movie.

00:07:51.400 --> 00:07:55.660
The overwhelming success of Christopher Nolan's
The Dark Knight convinced Warner Brothers

00:07:55.660 --> 00:08:00.200
that a Justice League - with a different Batman
- simply wouldn't work.

00:08:00.200 --> 00:08:04.090
Justice League: Mortal is the Justice League
movie that never was..

00:08:04.090 --> 00:08:08.449
So when you're watching Ben Affleck throw
down with Khal Drogo, just think about how

00:08:08.449 --> 00:08:13.180
much crazier it would've been if the director
of Mad Max had gotten to the League first…

00:08:14.820 --> 00:08:15.820
"Yeah!"

00:08:18.800 --> 00:08:19.860
"Oh, what a day!

00:08:20.200 --> 00:08:21.920
What a lovely day!"

00:08:22.780 --> 00:08:23.780
Thanks for watching!

00:08:23.790 --> 00:08:26.710
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
